#### Time complexity

$$T(n) = 2T(n/2) + 2$$

Using the master theorem (with $T(2) = 1$):

$$T(n) = \frac{3n}{2} - 2 \;\Rightarrow\; O(n)$$

#### Space complexity

$O(\log n)$ (recursion stack).

Use **binary search**; the function returns the smallest index holding 1 in `A`.

```cpp
int firstOne(int A[], int n) {
    if (A[0] == 1) return 0;
    else if (A[n-1] == 0) return -1;      // no 1 in the array
    else {
        int left = 0, right = n - 1, mid = 0;
        bool isFound = false;
        while (!isFound) {
            mid = (left + right) / 2;
            if (A[mid] == 0)
                left = mid + 1;
            else {
                if (A[mid-1] == 0) isFound = true;
                else right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
        return mid;
    }
}
```

Observation: for any middle element, either the left half or the right half of the array is sorted. This is used to do a binary search in $O(\log n)$.

```cpp
int BinarySearch(int a[], int target, int n) {
    int lo = 0, hi = n - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) return mid;
        if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {
            // left side is sorted
            if (target >= a[lo] && target <= a[mid])   // target on the left?
                hi = mid - 1;
            else
                lo = mid + 1;
        } else {
            // right side is sorted
            if (target >= a[mid] && target <= a[hi])   // target on the right?
                lo = mid + 1;
            else
                hi = mid - 1;
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```

The array is sorted and has distinct integers.

Check whether the value at the middle index equals the index itself, and return it if true. Otherwise, if the value at the middle index is greater than the index, recursively search the left half; else recursively search the right half.

```cpp
int func(vector<int>& A, int lo, int hi) {
    if (lo > hi) return -1;          // base case
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (A[mid] == mid)
        return mid;
    else if (A[mid] > mid)
        return func(A, lo, mid - 1);
    else
        return func(A, mid + 1, hi);
}
```

**Example:** call `func({-10, -5, 0, 3, 7}, 0, 4)`.

1. Main call: `mid = 2`, `A[2] = 0 != 2`. Since $0 < 2$, search the right half.
2. `func(A, 3, 4)`: `mid = 3`, `A[3] = 3`, equal to the index, so return 3.

#### Algorithm

1. Let $J$ be an empty sequence of jumps.
2. Set the current position $c = 0$.
3. While $c < n$:
   1. Find the furthest lilypad $l$ reachable from $c$ within the next $r$ units (not beyond $n$).
   2. Add a jump from $c$ to $l$'s position to $J$.
   3. Set $c$ to $l$'s location.
4. The result is the path $J$.

#### Code

```cpp
// lilypads[i] is nonzero if there is a lilypad at position i
vector<int> minJumps(vector<int> lilypads, int n, int r) {
    vector<int> path = {0};
    int current_position = 0;
    while (current_position != n) {
        int max_possible = current_position;
        for (int i = current_position + 1; i <= current_position + r && i <= n; i++) {
            if (lilypads[i]) {
                max_possible = i;
            }
        }
        path.push_back(max_possible);
        current_position = max_possible;
    }
    return path;
}
```

