**Definition.** $\text{Merge}(i,j)$ is true iff $z_1\dots z_{i+j}$ is a merge (interleaving) of $x_1\dots x_i$ and $y_1\dots y_j$.

**Recurrence.** Consider the last character $z_{i+j}$ of the prefix. It must come from the end of $x$-prefix or the end of $y$-prefix:

$$
\text{Merge}(i,j)=
\begin{cases}
\text{true} & i=0,\ j=0\\[2pt]
\big(i>0 \wedge x_i = z_{i+j} \wedge \text{Merge}(i-1,j)\big)\ \vee\ \big(j>0 \wedge y_j = z_{i+j} \wedge \text{Merge}(i,j-1)\big) & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
$$

(So $\text{Merge}(i,0)$ is true iff $x_1..x_i = z_1..z_i$, and $\text{Merge}(0,j)$ is true iff $y_1..y_j = z_1..z_j$.) The answer to the problem is $\text{Merge}(n,m)$.

**Code (bottom-up dynamic programming).**

```python
def is_merge(x, y, z):
    n, m = len(x), len(y)
    if len(z) != n + m:
        return False
    M = [[False] * (m + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]
    M[0][0] = True
    for i in range(n + 1):
        for j in range(m + 1):
            if i == 0 and j == 0:
                continue
            ok = False
            if i > 0 and x[i-1] == z[i+j-1] and M[i-1][j]:
                ok = True
            if j > 0 and y[j-1] == z[i+j-1] and M[i][j-1]:
                ok = True
            M[i][j] = ok
    return M[n][m]
```

**Complexity.** There are $(n+1)(m+1)$ subproblems, each solved in $O(1)$: time $O(nm)$, space $O(nm)$ (reducible to $O(\min(n,m))$ by keeping one row). A plain recursion without memoization would be exponential because the same $(i,j)$ is recomputed many times.

