**Code:**

```c
int MCMBU(int arr[], int n) {
    int Strg[n][n];
    for (int slide = 1; slide < n; slide++) {
        for (int si = 0; si < n - slide; si++) {
            int ei = si + slide;
            if (si + 1 == ei) Strg[si][ei] = 0;
            else {
                int min = INT_MAX;
                for (int k = si + 1; k < ei; k++) {
                    int fp = Strg[si][k];
                    int sp = Strg[k][ei];
                    int sw = arr[si] * arr[k] * arr[ei];
                    int tot = fp + sp + sw;
                    if (tot < min) min = tot;
                }
                Strg[si][ei] = min;
            }
        }
    }
    return Strg[0][n - 1];
}
```

**Algorithm (Floyd-Warshall)**

1. Initialize the distance matrix:
   - create a 2D array `dist` of size $n \times n$, where $n$ is the number of vertices;
   - set the diagonal $dist[i][i] = 0$;
   - for each edge $(i, j)$, set $dist[i][j]$ (and $dist[j][i]$ if the graph is undirected) to the given weight; all other entries are $\infty$.
2. Update the distance matrix: for each vertex $k$, consider all pairs $(i, j)$:
   - if the path through $k$ is shorter than the current one, i.e. $dist[i][k] + dist[k][j] < dist[i][j]$, update $dist[i][j] = dist[i][k] + dist[k][j]$;
   - otherwise skip.
3. Return the `dist` matrix, which holds the all-pairs shortest paths (APSP).

The problem leads to the following recurrence relation:

$$P(i,j) = p\,P(i-1,j) + q\,P(i,j-1) \quad \text{for all } i, j > 0$$

Base conditions:

$$P(0,j) = 1 \ \ \forall j > 0, \qquad P(i,0) = 0 \ \ \forall i > 0$$

**Pseudo code**

```
WORLD-SERIES(n, p) {
    q = 1 - p;
    for (j = 1 to n) {
        P[0][j] = 1;
    }
    for (i = 1 to n) {
        P[i][0] = 0;
        for (j = 1 to n) {
            P[i][j] = p * P[i-1][j] + q * P[i][j-1];   // using the recurrence
        }
    }
    return P[n][n];
}
```

**Complexity**

Space complexity $= O(n^2)$ and time complexity $= O(n^2)$, since a table of size $(n+1) \times (n+1)$ is computed.

